1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,848
Thursday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:01,898 --> 00:00:09,720
Somebody opens the marketplace web page, types Aziz's phone number, and starts to try passwords from a leaked list, one after another.

3
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:11,592
Aziz is asleep.

4
00:00:11,642 --> 00:00:13,752
His password is in the platform.

5
00:00:13,802 --> 00:00:19,976
So how does a platform keep a password that it must check, but can never read?

6
00:00:20,026 --> 00:00:25,928
In Lesson 10, Aziz typed his password two times, and the platform made him a customer.

7
00:00:25,978 --> 00:00:30,248
Lessons eleven and twelve guarded the code that comes after the password.

8
00:00:30,298 --> 00:00:33,872
This lesson opens the box that holds the password itself.

9
00:00:33,922 --> 00:00:35,624
First, where it lives.

10
00:00:35,674 --> 00:00:42,944
Aziz has one password for every app of the bank, so the platform keeps exactly one row for him, in Postgres.

11
00:00:42,994 --> 00:00:46,280
It belongs to the customer gateway, not to Keycloak.

12
00:00:46,330 --> 00:00:48,608
Keycloak never sees the password at all.

13
00:00:48,658 --> 00:00:50,360
Why not let Keycloak keep it?

14
00:00:50,410 --> 00:00:54,224
Because the gateway must check the password before it sends any code.

15
00:00:54,274 --> 00:01:00,944
If Keycloak held it, the code would go out first, and the password would travel to Keycloak at every login.

16
00:01:00,994 --> 00:01:02,816
Now, what is in the row.

17
00:01:02,866 --> 00:01:04,688
Not the password.

18
00:01:04,738 --> 00:01:10,064
The gateway runs the password through Argon2id, a memory-hard hashing function.

19
00:01:10,114 --> 00:01:15,128
Memory-hard means that every single check needs a real block of memory and real time.

20
00:01:15,178 --> 00:01:18,056
For the gateway, that is one check per login.

21
00:01:18,106 --> 00:01:27,032
For an attacker with a stolen copy of the table, every guess costs the same, so millions of guesses become very slow and very expensive.

22
00:01:27,082 --> 00:01:33,200
Before the hashing, the gateway adds a salt: random bytes, new for each password.

23
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:39,920
So two customers with the same password still get two different digests, and no table of ready answers works.

24
00:01:39,970 --> 00:01:46,016
The row keeps one string: the name of the algorithm, its settings, the salt and the digest.

25
00:01:46,066 --> 00:01:50,072
The settings come from the configuration of the installation, not from the code.

26
00:01:50,122 --> 00:01:55,208
Older digests are never kept, and only the login module may read the row.

27
00:01:55,258 --> 00:02:00,440
Older functions like bcrypt were not chosen, because they are not memory-hard.

28
00:02:00,490 --> 00:02:02,096
Back to Thursday night.

29
00:02:02,146 --> 00:02:08,552
Every login starts with the password, before a code, before a new-device request, before a web approval.

30
00:02:08,602 --> 00:02:11,816
The gateway compares the digests in constant time.

31
00:02:11,866 --> 00:02:13,808
And here is a detail I really like.

32
00:02:13,858 --> 00:02:19,976
When the number belongs to nobody, the gateway still runs a check, against a fixed dummy digest.

33
00:02:20,026 --> 00:02:24,704
The answer is the same, wrong number or password, and it takes the same time.

34
00:02:24,754 --> 00:02:28,424
So the stranger cannot even learn whether a customer exists.

35
00:02:28,474 --> 00:02:31,664
Each wrong password adds one to a counter in the row.

36
00:02:31,714 --> 00:02:37,160
The same transaction writes an audit event and an event for the bank's antifraud system.

37
00:02:37,210 --> 00:02:41,912
At the configured limit, the gateway locks password login for the configured time.

38
00:02:41,962 --> 00:02:46,736
Aziz gets a message by SMS and by push: password login locked.

39
00:02:46,786 --> 00:02:50,360
The stranger gets one answer: Too many wrong passwords.

40
00:02:50,410 --> 00:02:53,768
Try again at the given time, or reset the password.

41
00:02:53,818 --> 00:02:57,344
On Friday morning, Aziz opens the mobile bank.

42
00:02:57,394 --> 00:02:59,216
It works.

43
00:02:59,266 --> 00:03:01,088
A lock is not a block.

44
00:03:01,138 --> 00:03:05,648
His sessions keep running, because renewing a session needs no password.

45
00:03:05,698 --> 00:03:12,248
The lock ends by itself after the lock time, or after a password reset, which Lesson 30 explains.

46
00:03:12,298 --> 00:03:15,752
But the message worries Aziz, so he changes his password.

47
00:03:15,802 --> 00:03:21,224
In the mobile bank, he types the current password, then the new one, and then the new one again.

48
00:03:21,274 --> 00:03:27,920
The same rules as at registration apply: long enough, not on the breached list, no phone number inside.

49
00:03:27,970 --> 00:03:32,072
A wrong current password would count as one more wrong entry.

50
00:03:32,122 --> 00:03:37,448
In one transaction, the platform writes the new digest and removes the old one.

51
00:03:37,498 --> 00:03:40,208
The counter goes to zero and the lock ends.

52
00:03:40,258 --> 00:03:44,912
The events go to the outbox in the same transaction, so nothing is half done.

53
00:03:44,962 --> 00:03:49,592
Then every other session of the same or a lower rank than the one he used ends.

54
00:03:49,642 --> 00:03:52,976
His marketplace session on the laptop ends at once.

55
00:03:53,026 --> 00:03:55,544
He logs in there again with the new password.

56
00:03:55,594 --> 00:03:58,448
And a message goes out: password changed.

57
00:03:58,498 --> 00:04:00,944
A few things never happen to a password.

58
00:04:00,994 --> 00:04:05,120
The platform never shows it, returns it, logs it or sends it.

59
00:04:05,170 --> 00:04:07,976
The app masks it on the screen and does not keep it.

60
00:04:08,026 --> 00:04:12,056
And it is not the app PIN, which only opens the app on his phone.

61
00:04:12,106 --> 00:04:13,928
To recap.

62
00:04:13,978 --> 00:04:19,856
One row for each customer, with a slow, salted Argon2id digest.

63
00:04:19,906 --> 00:04:25,712
The password is checked first, in constant time, even for a number that belongs to nobody.

64
00:04:25,762 --> 00:04:29,768
Wrong guesses lock password login, but they block nothing else.

65
00:04:29,818 --> 00:04:32,408
A change ends the weaker sessions.

66
00:04:32,458 --> 00:04:37,040
Aziz now has a number, a password and a session, all at level one.

67
00:04:37,090 --> 00:04:39,992
The bank knows his phone, but not yet the person.

68
00:04:40,042 --> 00:04:43,520
To prove the person, the platform must call MyID.

69
00:04:43,570 --> 00:04:47,432
But before it may call MyID, what must Aziz agree to?

70
00:04:47,482 --> 00:04:49,304
Next: consent.
